From: genshanxinli <127909456+genshanxinli@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 11:20:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: reserve per-band token quota

The token pool is global: mt76_token_consume() blocks every band once
dev->token_count reaches token_size - MT76_TOKEN_FREE_THR. A 4SS 6 GHz
burst can therefore occupy the whole pool and leave the 5 GHz and 2.4 GHz
bands without a token to send from, even though those bands only need a
handful.

Account the in-flight tokens per band and add token_reserved[band], a
configurable reservation (default 0). While another band has traffic in
flight, or scheduled or queued a frame within the last 500 ms, its
reservation is subtracted from the pool this band may use, so a burst
cannot take the reserved entries. A band whose remaining share would fall
inside the free headroom keeps the shared pool instead, so a band can
always leave the blocked state on the next completion. An increase must
leave two free thresholds in the pool; a decrease or clearing is always
accepted, so a reservation can be backed out after the pool shrank.

The blocked state is tracked per band instead of being set and cleared
for all bands together: the TX scheduler sees a per-band blocked flag, so
the band that reached its ceiling waits for completions while a band that
still has pool entries keeps scheduling. A reservation is released as
soon as its band goes idle, so a single active band still uses the whole
pool. The driver's unblock request clears the per-band flags and always
reschedules the TX worker in quota mode, because the release path may
already have cleared the flag that made progress; a release without a
wake pointer, such as a failed TX preparation returning its token,
refreshes and reschedules on its own.

With no reservation configured the original __mt76_set_tx_blocked() body
runs verbatim, so the default keeps the global threshold and the old
set/clear behavior; the per-band blocked counter is only maintained while
the quota is on.

Expose a 0600 debugfs file "token_quota" next to "token_info" with the
per-band used/reserved/blocked counters:

  band |   used | reserved | blocked
     0 |     12 |        0 |       0
     1 |      8 |      512 |       3
     2 |   8000 |        0 |     127

  pool: 8020 of 16384 in flight, quota on

Writing "<band> <tokens>" sets one reservation, e.g. "echo '1 512' >
token_quota"; writing 0 to every band returns to the default. The
counters and the reset cleanup are maintained under token_lock, and no
AQL, firmware or pool size is changed.

The patch applies after the existing 0001..0015 series and builds
mt76.ko, mt76-connac-lib.ko, mt7996e.ko (NPU config) and mt7925-common.ko
against the target 7.3 kernel tree. The 5 GHz blocked-counter A/B on
hardware is left for a follow-up, as the issue says.

Signed-off-by: genshanxinli <127909456+genshanxinli@users.noreply.github.com>
---
--- a/mt76.h
+++ b/mt76.h
@@ -1019,6 +1019,20 @@ struct mt76_dev {
 	u16 token_start;
 	u16 token_size;
 
+	/*
+	 * Per-band token quota. @token_reserved holds the number of pool
+	 * entries that a band may reclaim from the other bands while it is
+	 * active; everything is shared when it is zero. @token_band_used
+	 * tracks in-flight tokens per band, @token_band_blocked counts the
+	 * transitions into the blocked state and @token_band_active keeps
+	 * the last time a band scheduled or queued a frame.
+	 */
+	u16 token_reserved[__MT_MAX_BAND];
+	u16 token_band_used[__MT_MAX_BAND];
+	u32 token_band_blocked[__MT_MAX_BAND];
+	unsigned long token_band_active[__MT_MAX_BAND];
+	bool token_quota;
+
 	spinlock_t rx_token_lock;
 	struct idr rx_token;
 	u16 rx_token_size;
@@ -2126,6 +2140,8 @@ struct mt76_txwi_cache *
 mt76_token_release(struct mt76_dev *dev, int token, bool *wake);
 int mt76_token_consume(struct mt76_dev *dev, struct mt76_txwi_cache **ptxwi);
 void __mt76_set_tx_blocked(struct mt76_dev *dev, bool blocked);
+int mt76_token_set_reserved(struct mt76_dev *dev, unsigned int band,
+			    unsigned int reserved);
 struct mt76_txwi_cache *mt76_rx_token_release(struct mt76_dev *dev, int token);
 int mt76_rx_token_consume(struct mt76_dev *dev, void *ptr,
 			  struct mt76_txwi_cache *r, dma_addr_t phys);
